# The capture-cost store is append-only: one reading per run of `headwater new`,
# and no line is ever rewritten. Two branches that each scaffolded a document
# both hold a reading the other does not, and git's default merge reports that
# as a conflict on the last line of the file. `union` keeps both sides' lines,
# which is the correct resolution every time for a file of this shape.
#
# It is safe here and nowhere else in this repository: `union` on a file whose
# lines depend on each other produces a result that neither side wrote. A
# reading depends on no other reading, and `headwater capture` reports a line it
# cannot read rather than trusting the file.
.headwater/capture-cost.jsonl merge=union

# The adoption store is the same shape and it holds under the same argument: one
# reading per run of `headwater taxonomy audit --record`, no line ever
# rewritten, and a reading that depends on no other reading. `taxonomy audit`
# reports a line it cannot read rather than trusting the file.
#
# It carries one case the store above cannot produce: two branches that each
# recorded a reading of one tree at one date under one lock write the same line,
# and `union` keeps both copies. Nothing rewrites a store, so the duplicate
# stands until a person deletes it. What the writer's `(lock, date)` refusal
# holds is the other direction: no later run adds a third copy.
.headwater/adoption.jsonl merge=union

# --- derived artifacts, which no merge may reconcile ------------------------
#
# Everything below holds a fold: a count over the whole corpus, or a digest over
# the whole canonical text. The two stores above are the opposite shape, and the
# contrast is the whole rule. A line of an append-only store depends on no other
# line, so two branches that each add one may both be kept and `union` keeps
# them. A fold depends on every line, so two branches that each add one document
# both rewrite the same total to the same value. A three-way merge reads that as
# one change written twice, takes it with no conflict, and leaves a number that
# was true on neither branch and is true of nothing.
#
# **The driver below does not reach that case, and #717 owns it.** Measured
# rather than argued, in `engine/crates/census/tests/merge_driver.rs`: a merge
# driver is a content merge, git compares blob identifiers first, and two
# branches that wrote one byte string give it one blob, which it takes at the
# tree level with no driver consulted. So what the driver reaches is two
# branches that moved a fold to *different* values, which is the common case
# whenever they add documents of different kinds, and what covers the rest is a
# check on the merged state: CI runs each producer with `--check` against
# `refs/pull/N/merge`, which is the union.
#
# A record that decomposes into one line per entity escapes this, and where the
# decomposition is affordable this repository takes it rather than listing the
# file here. `engine/crates/census/fixtures/corpus.census` holds one record per
# file and no total, and `engine/crates/graph/fixtures/corpus.graph` holds one
# record per citation rather than a count per anchor. Both merge, and both still
# assert every number they used to state, because a total is a function of the
# records it counts.
#
# What is listed here is the remainder: the folds whose decomposition costs more
# than it returns. `corpus.checks` is the case that fixes the price. One record
# per check instance would be a five-thousand-line file that moves on every
# commit, which is a file nobody reads, and the count it would replace moves on
# every commit anyway.
#
# `merge=headwater-regenerate` needs one command per clone, which git will not
# take from a repository because a merge driver is an executable:
#
#     git config merge.headwater-regenerate.name "regenerate a derived artifact"
#     git config merge.headwater-regenerate.driver ".githooks/merge-regenerate %O %A %B %P"
#
# Without it git falls back to an ordinary merge and the anomaly above is
# available again, so `.githooks/pre-commit` reports a clone that has not run it.
# The driver refuses rather than reconciling, and `.githooks/merge-regenerate`
# says why it cannot regenerate during a merge.
#
# **This reaches a local merge and a rebase, and it does not reach the merge
# button on the forge.** A server-side merge runs no driver from this file. What
# covers that is the pull-request run, which builds `refs/pull/N/merge` and holds
# each of these artifacts with `--check`, and that run is only conclusive while
# the branch is current with `main`.

.headwater/corpus.json merge=headwater-regenerate
.headwater/export.json merge=headwater-regenerate
.headwater/nav.yml merge=headwater-regenerate
.headwater/taxonomy.lock merge=headwater-regenerate
engine/crates/check/fixtures/corpus.checks merge=headwater-regenerate
engine/crates/lock/fixtures/corpus.lock merge=headwater-regenerate

# The generated documents, which are the same shape for the same reason. Each
# one opens with a count of the shelf below it, and `headwater generate` writes
# every one of them from the corpus. Two branches that each add a decision both
# write `48` where the merged tree holds 49.
#
# They are listed rather than matched. `docs/*/README.md` would reach
# `docs/taxonomies/README.md` and `docs/w3id/README.md`, which nobody generates
# and somebody edits by hand, and refusing a merge of a hand-authored file is a
# worse failure than the one this list prevents. A list goes stale instead, so
# `the_generated_documents_are_declared_unmergeable` in the census crate holds
# every row the census calls generated against this block.
docs/acceptance-criteria/README.md merge=headwater-regenerate
docs/decisions/README.md merge=headwater-regenerate
docs/evaluations/README.md merge=headwater-regenerate
docs/interfaces/README.md merge=headwater-regenerate
docs/obligations/README.md merge=headwater-regenerate
docs/probes/README.md merge=headwater-regenerate
docs/process/decisions/README.md merge=headwater-regenerate
docs/requirements/README.md merge=headwater-regenerate
docs/reviews/README.md merge=headwater-regenerate
docs/spec/09-open-questions.md merge=headwater-regenerate
docs/spec/README.md merge=headwater-regenerate
docs/tutorials/README.md merge=headwater-regenerate

# The hand-built pages that carry a measured figure. `tools/refresh-figures.sh`
# substitutes 34 measured keys into every element marked `data-figure` on every
# page under `site/`, 107 occurrences across these three, and
# `.githooks/pre-commit` refuses a commit that leaves one stale. So every branch
# that adds a document rewrites all three of these files.
#
# The reason they are here is not the reason above, and the difference is worth
# stating because these pages look like the case that escapes. They are already
# decomposed: one element per figure, 57 of them on the landing page, so two
# branches that move *different* figures merge correctly and nothing in this
# block would be needed. What survives the decomposition is two branches that
# move the *same* figure to the *same* new value. Both add a document, both
# write `census.seen">426`, and the union is 427.
#
# Half of that case is reached from here and half is not, per the note above.
# Two branches adding documents of *different* kinds move different figures, so
# the two pages differ, the driver is called and it refuses. Two branches adding
# documents of the *same* kind write one identical page, git takes it at the
# tree level, and no driver runs. #717 owns the second half for every path in
# this file.
#
# Listed rather than matched, and the list is enumerated rather than remembered.
# `site/*` would reach `site/tutorial/index.html`, whose 43 verbatim blocks are
# copied out of the tutorial document rather than measured, and the four pages
# under `site/changelog/`, `site/compare/`, `site/glossary/` and `site/ns/`,
# which carry no figure at all. Refusing a merge of a page somebody edits by
# hand is the worse of the two failures, so
# `every_page_carrying_a_figure_is_declared_unmergeable` in the census crate
# reads every page under `site/` and holds the ones carrying a figure against
# this block, in both directions.
#
# One cost, which is intended rather than a regression:
# `tools/refresh-site-tokens.sh` also writes into these three pages, copying one
# identical block into every page under `site/`. Two branches that both
# refreshed the tokens wrote the same bytes and used to merge, and now they stop
# and ask. The remedy the driver names rewrites the figures and leaves the token
# block untouched, so the resolution is one command either way.
site/how-it-works/index.html merge=headwater-regenerate
site/index.html merge=headwater-regenerate
site/proof/index.html merge=headwater-regenerate
